{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00763 - MONDO_5364",
  "verbalization": "Methimazole is proposed to be associated with Graves Disease through a mechanistic pathway involving thyrotoxicosis and dibromotyrosine. The evidence suggests that Methimazole exerts its therapeutic effect by inhibiting thyroid hormone synthesis, leading to thyrotoxicosis, which in turn promotes the formation of dibromotyrosine, a marker of hyperthyroidism. This pathway is corroborated by multiple independent evidence chains, each linking Methimazole to thyrotoxicosis and ultimately to Graves Disease. The shared involvement of dibromotyrosine across different pathways strengthens the hypothesis that Methimazole modulates thyroid function through a common intermediate. The integration of these pathways supports the notion that Methimazole may repurpose its therapeutic effect to treat Graves Disease by targeting the underlying biochemical processes that drive thyrotoxicosis.",
